A Bool attribute set explicitly to undef comes back as 0 and serializes as
`false`, so "I do not know / leave it unset" turns into an explicit false on
the wire. Measured on 4887f658:

  IO::K8s::Api::Core::V1::SecurityContext->new(privileged => undef)->TO_JSON
  # {"privileged":false}          <-- expected {}

  IO::K8s::Api::Core::V1::SecurityContext->new->TO_JSON
  # {}                            correct

  $k8s->struct_to_object('Api::Core::V1::SecurityContext',
                         { privileged => undef })->TO_JSON
  # {}                            correct

The inflation path is fine -- _inflate_struct skips undef values outright
(lib/IO/K8s.pm:898, `next unless defined $value`), so a JSON null never
reaches the coercer and the attribute stays unset. Only the CONSTRUCTOR path
is affected, via _normalize_bool's `return 0 unless defined $value`
(lib/IO/K8s/Resource.pm:133).

The attribute's isa is Maybe[Bool], so undef is a legal value that the
coercer destroys before the type ever sees it. TO_JSON's `next unless
defined $value` (lib/IO/K8s/Role/Resource.pm:113) would omit the field
correctly if undef survived.

NOT a regression from karr #37. The coercer it replaced --
`sub { ref $_[0] ? (${$_[0]} ? 1 : 0) : ($_[0] ? 1 : 0) }` -- mapped undef
to 0 as well. This is long-standing, and it is being written down now
because karr #37 made _normalize_bool the single place where the answer is
decided, which is also the single place where it can be changed.

WHY IT MATTERS
The idiom it breaks is ordinary Perl:

  SecurityContext->new(privileged => $config->{privileged});

With the key absent, the caller gets `privileged: false` in the manifest
instead of an unset field. On a strategic-merge patch or a server-side
apply, an explicit false is a claim of ownership over the field and
overrides a value the cluster or an admission controller set; an omitted
field is not. Same class of silent-wrong-answer as karr #37, opposite
direction.

WHAT TO DO -- decide first, then implement
  * Change `return 0 unless defined $value` to `return undef unless
    defined $value` in _normalize_bool. Check the deref case first: after
    `$value = $$value if ref $value`, a \undef also lands here, and \undef
    is not a false idiom -- decide whether it becomes undef or stays 0, and
    say why.
  * Confirm every Bool attribute really is Maybe[Bool] and not a required
    Bool anywhere; a required one would start failing its type check on an
    explicit undef, which may be the RIGHT answer but must be a deliberate
    one. (Resource.pm:_k8s builds `$required ? $type_spec : Maybe[$type_spec]`,
    so required Bools exist in principle -- count them before deciding.)
  * is_array_of_bool: the array coercer maps _normalize_bool over elements
    (Resource.pm:264). An undef ELEMENT becoming undef inside an ArrayRef
    [Bool] is a different question from an undef attribute -- ArrayRef[Bool]
    may reject it. Decide explicitly; do not let it fall out.
  * Back-compat: callers who passed undef and relied on getting 0 back from
    the accessor change behaviour. Changes entry states it.

